{{also|Back|back-|bäck}}

==English==
{{wikipedia|back|back (rear of the body)|mul=back (disambiguation)|mullabel=other uses}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|en|/bæk/|[bæk]|[bak]|[-k̚]|[-ˀk]}}
* {{a|{{w|Scouse}}}} {{IPA|en|[bax]}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-back.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|æk|s=1}}
* {{hyphenation|en|back}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|bak}}, from {{inh|en|ang|bæc}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*bak}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*baką}}, possibly from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*bʰeg-|t=to bend}}. The adverb represents an [[aphetic]] form of {{m|en|aback}}.

Compare {{cognate|gml|bak|t=back}}, from Old Saxon {{m|osx|bak}}, and {{cognate|fy|bekling|t=chair back}}, {{cognate|goh|bah}}, {{cognate|sv|-}} and {{cognate|no|bak}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|Bache||sow [adult female hog]}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|-|more}}

# At or near the [[rear]].
#: {{ux|en|Go in the '''back''' door of the house.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=19|passage=Nothing was too small to receive attention, if a supervising eye could suggest improvements likely to conduce to the common welfare. Mr. Gordon Burnage, for instance, personally visited dust-bins and '''back''' premises, accompanied by a sort of village bailiff, going his round like a commanding officer doing billets.}}
# {{lb|en|predicative}} Returned or restored to a [[previous]] place or condition.
#: {{ux|en|He was on vacation, but now he’s '''back'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The office fell into chaos when you left, but now order is '''back'''.}}
# Not current.
#: {{ux|en|I’d like to find a '''back''' issue of that magazine.}}
# Situated away from the main or most frequented areas.
#: {{ux|en|They took a '''back''' road.}}
#: {{ux|en|He lives out in the '''back''' country.}}
# In arrears; overdue.
#: {{ux|en|They still owe three months' '''back''' rent.}}
# Moving or operating [[backward]].
#: {{ux|en|'''back''' action}}
# {{senseid|en|phonetics}} {{lb|en|comparable|phonetics}} [[pronounce|Pronounced]] with the highest part of the body of the [[tongue]] toward the back of the [[mouth]], near the [[soft palate]] (most often describing a [[vowel]]).
#: {{ux|en|The vowel of {{smallcaps|lot}} has a back vowel in most dialects of England.}}

=====Usage notes=====
In linguistic use describing the position of the tongue, the comparative {{m|en|backer}} and superlative {{m|en|backest}} are usual; these may also be occasionally found for other senses, especially informally.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|near the rear}} {{l|en|rear}}
* {{sense|not current}} {{l|en|former}}, {{l|en|previous}}

=====Antonyms=====
* {{sense|near the rear|phonetics}} {{l|en|front}}
* {{sense|not current}} {{l|en|current}}
* {{sense|away from the main area}} {{l|en|main}} {{q|of roads}}

