# The rear of the [[body]], especially the part between the [[neck]] and the end of the [[spine]] and opposite the [[chest]] and [[belly]].
#: {{ux|en|Could you please scratch my '''back'''?}}
#* {{RQ:Jefferies Amateur Poacher|chapter=1|passage=It was not far from the house; but the ground sank into a depression there, and the ridge of it behind shut out everything except just the roof of the tallest hayrick. As one sat on the sward behind the elm, with the '''back''' turned on the rick and nothing in front but the tall elms and the oaks in the other hedge, it was quite easy to fancy it the verge of the prairie with the backwoods close by.}}
## The [[spine]] and associated tissues.
##: {{ux|en|I hurt my '''back''' lifting those crates.}}
## {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} Large and attractive [[buttock]]s.
##* {{quote-song|en|year=1992|lyricist=w:Sir Mix-A-Lot|title=Baby Got Back|passage=Take the average black man and ask him that.<br/>She gotta pack much '''back'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=w:George Pelecanos|title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=oHMFQdbIU_EC Right as Rain: A Novel]|isbn=0446610798| page=123| passage=He got his hand on her behind and caressed her firm, ample flesh.{{...}}"You got some '''back''' on you, girl."}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} The part of a piece of clothing which covers the back.
##: {{ux|en|I still need to finish the '''back''' of your dress.}}
## {{senseid|en|furniture backrest}} The [[backrest]], the part of a piece of furniture which receives the human back.
##: {{ux|en|Can you fix the '''back''' of this chair?}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} That part of the body that bears clothing. (Now used only in the phrase {{l|en|clothes on one's back}}.)
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1604|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=s:Measure for Measure| passage=Do thou but think / What 'tis to cram a maw or clothe a '''back''' / From such a filthy vice}}
# That which is [[farthest]] away from the front.
#: {{ux|en|He sat in the '''back''' of the room.}}
## The side of any object which is opposite the [[front]] or useful side.
##: {{ux|en|Turn the book over and look at the '''back'''.}}
### {{senseid|en|bound edge of book}} The edge of a book which is [[bind|bound]].
###: {{ux|en|The titles are printed on the '''backs''' of the books.}}
### {{lb|en|printing}} The inside [[margin]] of a page.
###* {{quote-book|en|year=1841|author={{w|William Savage (printer)|William Savage}}| page=472| year_published=1965|publisher=Ayer Publishing| title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=sS4H3hax5hAC A Dictionary of the Art of Printing]| isbn=0833731289| passage=Convenience and custom have familiarised us to the printed page being a little higher than the middle of the leaf, and to its having a little more margin at the fore edge than in the '''back'''.}}
### The side of a [[blade]] opposite the side used for cutting.
###: {{ux|en|Tap it with the '''back''' of your knife.}}
## The [[reverse]] side; the side that is not normally seen.
##: {{ux|en|I hung the clothes on the '''back''' of the door.}}
## Area behind, such as the [[backyard]] of a house.
##: {{ux|en|We'll meet out in the '''back''' of the library.}}
## The part of something that goes [[last]].
##: {{ux|en|The car was near the '''back''' of the train.}}
## {{lb|en|sports}} In some [[team sport]]s, a position behind most players on the team.
##: {{ux|en|The '''backs''' were lined up in an I formation.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 28, 2010|author=Kevin Darlin|work=BBC| title=[http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9308862.stm West Brom 1-3 Blackburn]| passage={{...}}Rovers were also aided by some poor defending from West Brom, whose lapses at the '''back''' undid their excellent work on the ball and condemned Roberto di Matteo's Baggies side to a third straight defeat.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} Upper part of a natural object which is considered to resemble an animal's back.
#: {{ux|en|The small boat raced over the '''backs''' of the waves.}}
# A support or resource in reserve.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|act=IV|scene=vii|passage=This project / Should have a '''back''' or second, that might hold, / If this should blast in proof.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The [[keel]] and [[keelson]] of a ship.
#: {{ux|en|The ship's '''back''' broke in the pounding surf.}}
# {{lb|en|mining}} The roof of a horizontal underground passage.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1911|author=Robert Bruce Brinsmade| title=[http://books.google.com/books?id=34VCAAAAIAAJ Mining Without Timber]| page=161| passage=The stope is kept full of broken ore, sufficient only being drawn to leave a working space between the floor of broken ore and the '''back''' of the stope.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} Effort, usually physical.
#: {{ux|en|Put some '''back''' into it!}}
# A non-alcoholic drink (often water or a soft drink), to go with hard liquor or a cocktail.
#: {{ux|en|Could I get a martini with a water '''back'''?}}
# Among [[leather]] [[dealer]]s, one of the thickest and stoutest tanned [[hide]]s.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1848|author=Maine Supreme Judicial Court|title=Maine Reports|volume=6|page=397
|passage={{...}}as delivered by a tanner the average weight of a '''back''' and two strips would be about 42 pounds{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|swimming}} {{clipping of|en|backstroke}}

